// Light sources for the flights dashboards. Each reads a small summary file (a few KB to 540 KB) built from the
// monthly flights files by build_summaries.sh (Malloy compiles the queries in build_summaries.malloy, DuckDB writes
// the rows once). The files hold plain counts and sums, so every rate and average here is a ratio of sums and comes
// out the same as the heavy sources in flights.malloy (flights, aircraft_legs, chain_slots), which stay for
// exploration. Rebuild and redeploy the files when the flights data or the method changes.
// Time filter: the summaries are monthly (daily for flights_daily), so dashboards filter on FLIGHT_MONTH, not PERIOD.
//
// Talk-to-your-data: these are the sources an AI should answer from. Each source, measure and "playbook" view
// (named after the buyer question it answers) says in its #" text what it means, its units, its caveats, and who
// acts on it. Common columns in the files: month_label ("June 2026"), month_start (date), carrier (BTS code, e.g.
// "AA"), airline ("American"), origin (departure airport code, e.g. "RDU"), size_band (origin size: "1 Hub (400+/day)"
// to "5 Very small (<10/day)"), dep_hour (scheduled local departure hour, 0-23), dest, route ("RDU → ATL").
// Refining a view: + { where: ... } keeps the view's ordering; + { limit: N } alone drops it (Malloy 0.2.56, friction
// log F59), so to shorten a playbook view add order_by as well, e.g. + { order_by: slot_rank limit: 10 }.

#" The airlines in the data (13; regional carriers such as SkyWest and Envoy appear under their own names, not the
#" mainline brand they fly for), with total scheduled flights May-July 2026 (n_flights). Use for filter values.
#" Spirit reported only 354 flights in these three months, so its totals are tiny by construction, not a sign of
#" reliability.
source: airline_list is motherduck.sql("""
  SELECT * FROM ltools.flights.flights_airlines
""") extend {
  primary_key: airline
}

#" One row per US departure airport (354): origin (code), origin_city, origin_state, size_band, departures and
#" departures_per_day (May-July 2026, 92 days), delay-chain minutes started there (chain_minutes = upper bound,
#" chain_minutes_next_leg = conservative), and latitude/longitude from OurAirports (public domain). Fast. Use it to
#" look up an airport's code, size band or city, or to rank airports by the delay-chain cost they start.
source: airport_list is motherduck.sql("""
  SELECT * FROM ltools.flights.flights_airports
""") extend {
  primary_key: origin
  dimension:
    #" "RDU · Raleigh/Durham, NC"
    airport_label is concat(origin, ' · ', origin_city)
    #" UPPER-BOUND delay-chain cost (US dollars, May-July 2026) started by late first flights from this airport:
    #" all later-flight late-aircraft minutes at $100.76 a minute (A4A 2024 block-minute cost).
    chain_cost_usd is chain_minutes * 100.76
    #" CONSERVATIVE delay-chain cost (US dollars, May-July 2026): next-flight-only minutes at $69.07 a minute (A4A 2025
    #" block-minute cost less fuel). Quote the two together as a range.
    chain_cost_conservative_usd is chain_minutes_next_leg * 69.07
  measure:
    #" Number of airports.
    airport_count is count()
}

#" START HERE for on-time, late-arrival, cancellation and delay-cause questions (fast: 33,542 pre-summed rows).
#" Every scheduled US domestic flight May-July 2026 (BTS on-time data, 1.85M flights), summed by month, airline,
#" departure airport (origin, size_band, and airport.* for city/state) and scheduled departure hour (dep_hour, local).
#" Gives the same numbers as the slow `flights` source. "Late" = arrived 15+ minutes after schedule (the BTS and DOT
#" definition). Delay causes are the airline-reported BTS categories, in hours. No destination here: for routes use
#" routes_summary or route_hours_summary.
source: delays_summary is motherduck.sql("""
  SELECT * FROM ltools.flights.flights_cube
""") extend {
  join_one: airport is airport_list on origin = airport.origin

  measure:
    #" Scheduled flights (including cancelled and diverted).
    flight_count is n_flights.sum()
    #" Cancelled flights.
    cancellations is n_cancelled.sum()
    #" Share of scheduled flights cancelled.
    # percent
    cancel_rate is cancellations / flight_count
    #" Share of operated flights (not cancelled or diverted) arriving 15+ minutes late. The main on-time measure:
    #" on-time rate = 1 - late_rate.
    # percent
    late_rate is n_late.sum() / n_operated.sum()
    #" Share of operated flights arriving less than 15 minutes after schedule (1 - late_rate; the BTS on-time rate).
    #" Cancelled and diverted flights are not in it: read it next to cancel_rate.
    # percent
    on_time_rate is 1 - late_rate
    #" Average arrival delay in minutes (operated flights; early arrivals count as negative, so it can look small).
    avg_arrival_delay is arr_delay_min_sum.sum() / n_arr_delay.sum()
    #" Delay hours BTS attributes to the airline itself (crew, maintenance, cleaning, fuelling, baggage).
    airline_delay_hours is carrier_delay_min_sum.sum() / 60
    #" Delay hours from significant weather.
    weather_delay_hours is weather_delay_min_sum.sum() / 60
    #" Delay hours from the national air system (air traffic control, airport operations, heavy traffic, non-extreme weather).
    air_traffic_delay_hours is nas_delay_min_sum.sum() / 60
    #" Delay hours from security (evacuations, screening lines).
    security_delay_hours is security_delay_min_sum.sum() / 60
    #" Delay hours because the aircraft arrived late from its previous flight (the delay-chain or knock-on cause).
    late_aircraft_delay_hours is late_aircraft_delay_min_sum.sum() / 60
    #" Of all cause-attributed delay hours, the share from the aircraft arriving late from its previous flight.
    # percent
    late_aircraft_share is late_aircraft_delay_min_sum.sum()
      / (carrier_delay_min_sum.sum() + weather_delay_min_sum.sum() + nas_delay_min_sum.sum()
         + security_delay_min_sum.sum() + late_aircraft_delay_min_sum.sum())
    #" Number of distinct departure airports.
    airport_count is count(origin)

  #" Reliability by airline (biggest first): late rate, cancel rate, average arrival delay.
  view: by_airline is {
    group_by: airline
    aggregate: flight_count, late_rate, cancel_rate, avg_arrival_delay
    order_by: flight_count desc
    limit: 15
  }

  #" Late rate by scheduled departure hour: delays build through the day.
  view: by_departure_hour is {
    group_by: dep_hour
    aggregate: late_rate, flight_count
    order_by: dep_hour
  }

  #" Delay hours by cause (whole selection).
  view: delay_causes is {
    aggregate: airline_delay_hours, late_aircraft_delay_hours, air_traffic_delay_hours, weather_delay_hours
  }

  #" The 20 busiest departure airports and their late rate.
  view: busiest_airports is {
    group_by: origin
    aggregate: flight_count, late_rate
    order_by: flight_count desc
    limit: 20
  }

  #" Reliability by departure-airport size band (hub to very small).
  view: by_size_band is {
    group_by: size_band
    aggregate: airport_count, flight_count, late_rate, cancel_rate, avg_arrival_delay, late_aircraft_share
    order_by: size_band
  }

  #" Delay hours by cause, per airport size band.
  view: delay_causes_by_size is {
    group_by: size_band
    aggregate: airline_delay_hours, late_aircraft_delay_hours, air_traffic_delay_hours, weather_delay_hours
    order_by: size_band
  }

  #" PLAYBOOK "Which airlines have the fewest delays?" Airlines with 100+ scheduled flights ranked by on-time arrival
  #" (fewest arrivals 15+ minutes late first), ranking first, with cancel_rate alongside because a cancelled flight is
  #" not counted as late (Republic ranks near the top on lateness but cancelled 4.8%). Nationally May-July 2026:
  #" Envoy 81.0% on time, Republic 80.6%, Alaska 80.1%, Delta 79.5%; Southwest last of the big airlines at 69.2%.
  #" Regional airlines (Envoy, Republic, SkyWest, PSA, Endeavor) rank under their own names, not the American Eagle /
  #" United Express / Delta Connection brand on the ticket. Spirit reported only 354 flights. For one airport filter the
  #" SOURCE first (a + { where } on this multi-stage view would filter the last stage):
  #" run: delays_summary extend { where: origin = 'RDU' } -> airlines_ranked_by_on_time. To shorten it, keep the order:
  #" + { order_by: ranking limit: 5 }. Who acts: travellers and corporate travel managers choosing an airline.
  #" Caveats: one summer season, not adjusted for weather, airlines fly different routes and hours (correlation, not cause).
  view: airlines_ranked_by_on_time is {
    group_by: airline
    aggregate: flight_count, on_time_rate, late_rate, cancel_rate
    having: flight_count >= 100
  } -> {
    group_by: airline, flight_count, on_time_rate, late_rate, cancel_rate
    calculate: ranking is rank()
    order_by: late_rate
  } -> {
    select: ranking, airline, flight_count, on_time_rate, late_rate, cancel_rate
    order_by: ranking
  }

  #" PLAYBOOK "When should I fly from <airport> (on <airline>) to avoid a delay?" Late rate by scheduled departure hour
  #" (local), hours with 100+ operated flights. Filter first, e.g.
  #" run: delays_summary -> best_time_to_fly_by_hour + { where: origin = 'RDU', airline = 'Delta' }. Nationally May-July
  #" 2026: 5am 7.6% late, 6am 8.9%, rising to about 38% for departures 6-9pm. At RDU: 5am 7.0%, 6am 9.9% vs 5pm 40.2%, 8pm 45.1%. This
  #" is the best time to FLY: BTS records no booking dates, so it says nothing about when to buy a ticket. Caveats: one
  #" summer, not adjusted for weather, correlation not cause (evening flights inherit the day's delays).
  view: best_time_to_fly_by_hour is {
    group_by: dep_hour
    aggregate:
      flight_count
      operated_flights is n_operated.sum()
      late_rate
      cancel_rate
    having: n_operated.sum() >= 100
    order_by: dep_hour
  }

  #" PLAYBOOK "What causes delays, and how much is a late-arriving aircraft?" Delay hours by cause and the
  #" late-aircraft share, nationally and by airport size band. Who acts: airline operations (late aircraft and
  #" airline causes are theirs to cut; air-traffic and weather mostly are not). A large late-aircraft share means
  #" delays are knock-ons from earlier flights: see chain_slots_summary -> departures_to_protect_first.
  view: what_causes_delays is {
    aggregate:
      flight_count, late_rate, airline_delay_hours, late_aircraft_delay_hours, air_traffic_delay_hours,
      weather_delay_hours, security_delay_hours, late_aircraft_share
    nest: by_size_band is {
      group_by: size_band
      aggregate: flight_count, late_rate, airline_delay_hours, late_aircraft_delay_hours, air_traffic_delay_hours,
        weather_delay_hours, late_aircraft_share
      order_by: size_band
    }
  }
}

#" Flights per calendar day, airline and departure airport (122,552 rows; fast), for day-by-day trends, day-of-week
#" questions (weekday) and finding bad days (storms, outages). Columns: flight_date, month_label, carrier, airline, origin, size_band.
source: daily_summary is motherduck.sql("""
  SELECT * FROM ltools.flights.flights_daily
""") extend {
  dimension:
    #" Day of the week of the flight date ("Mon" ... "Sun").
    weekday is
      pick 'Sun' when day_of_week(flight_date) = 1
      pick 'Mon' when day_of_week(flight_date) = 2
      pick 'Tue' when day_of_week(flight_date) = 3
      pick 'Wed' when day_of_week(flight_date) = 4
      pick 'Thu' when day_of_week(flight_date) = 5
      pick 'Fri' when day_of_week(flight_date) = 6
      else 'Sat'
    #" Day of the week as a number for sorting, 1 = Sunday ... 7 = Saturday.
    weekday_number is day_of_week(flight_date)
  measure:
    #" Scheduled flights.
    flight_count is n_flights.sum()
    #" Cancelled flights.
    cancellations is n_cancelled.sum()
    #" Share of operated flights arriving 15+ minutes late.
    # percent
    late_rate is n_late.sum() / n_operated.sum()

  #" PLAYBOOK "Which day of the week is most reliable to fly?" Late and cancel rates by weekday. Filter to an airport
  #" with + { where: origin = 'RDU' }. Who acts: travellers and corporate travel managers choosing a travel day.
  #" Nationally May-July 2026: Tuesday and Saturday 22.2% late, Sunday 27.4% and Monday 27.2% worst. Caveat: each
  #" weekday occurs only 13 times (Friday 14), so a few stormy days move a day's rate; the gaps are smaller than by hour.
  view: best_day_of_week is {
    group_by: weekday, weekday_number
    aggregate:
      flight_count, late_rate
      # percent
      cancel_rate is cancellations / flight_count
    order_by: weekday_number
  }

  #" Flights, late rate and cancellations per day.
  view: by_day is {
    group_by: flight_date
    aggregate: flight_count, late_rate, cancellations
    order_by: flight_date
  }
}

#" The calendar months in the data, for the Month filter.
source: month_list is daily_summary -> {
  group_by: month_label
  aggregate: first_day is flight_date.min()
  order_by: first_day
}

#" START HERE for airport comparisons, small airport vs hub, and the best hour to depart from an airport (fast).
#" The same month x airline x departure airport x scheduled hour rows as delays_summary, with the aircraft-day
#" measures: each flight is placed in its aircraft's day (tail number), so it knows whether it is the aircraft's first
#" flight of the day and whether the previous flight was late. late_rate here counts flights that operated and have a
#" tail number (within 0.1 point of delays_summary). Same numbers as the slow `aircraft_legs` source.
source: legs_summary is motherduck.sql("""
  SELECT * FROM ltools.flights.flights_cube
""") extend {
  join_one: airport is airport_list on origin = airport.origin

  measure:
    #" Scheduled flights.
    flight_count is n_flights.sum()
    #" Share of scheduled flights cancelled.
    # percent
    cancel_rate is n_cancelled.sum() / n_flights.sum()
    #" Flights that operated and have a tail number (the base of late_rate).
    legs is n_legs.sum()
    #" Of those, flights that arrived 15+ minutes late.
    late_legs is n_late_legs.sum()
    #" Share of operated flights arriving 15+ minutes late.
    # percent
    late_rate is late_legs / legs
    #" Average arrival delay in minutes (early arrivals count as negative).
    avg_arrival_delay is arr_delay_min_sum.sum() / n_arr_delay.sum()
    #" Share of an airport's departures that are the aircraft's first flight of the day. First flights leave with a
    #" fresh aircraft, so airports with a high share (small airports where planes overnight) start the day on time.
    # percent
    first_flight_share is n_first_legs.sum() / legs
    #" Of the flights that arrived late, the share whose aircraft's previous flight that day was also late.
    # percent
    chain_share is n_chain_late_legs.sum() / late_legs
    #" Of cause-attributed delay hours, the share from the aircraft arriving late from its previous flight.
    # percent
    late_aircraft_share is late_aircraft_delay_min_sum.sum()
      / (carrier_delay_min_sum.sum() + weather_delay_min_sum.sum() + nas_delay_min_sum.sum()
         + security_delay_min_sum.sum() + late_aircraft_delay_min_sum.sum())

  #" Delay-chain exposure by airport size band.
  view: chain_by_size is {
    group_by: size_band
    aggregate: legs, late_rate, cancel_rate, first_flight_share, chain_share, late_aircraft_share
    order_by: size_band
  }

  #" Late rate by scheduled hour, per airport size band (5am onwards).
  view: hour_by_size is {
    where: dep_hour >= 5
    group_by: dep_hour, size_band
    aggregate: late_rate
    having: legs > 0
    order_by: dep_hour
  }

  #" Late rate by scheduled departure hour.
  view: by_hour is {
    group_by: dep_hour
    aggregate: late_rate, legs
    having: legs > 0
    order_by: dep_hour
  }

  #" Airlines (biggest first) with late and cancel rates.
  view: by_airline is {
    group_by: airline
    aggregate: flight_count, late_rate, cancel_rate, avg_arrival_delay
    order_by: flight_count desc
    limit: 10
  }

  #" PLAYBOOK "Is it better to fly out of a small airport or a hub?" Reliability by departure-airport size band:
  #" late rate, cancel rate, average delay, the share of departures that are the aircraft's first flight of the day,
  #" and the share of late flights whose previous flight was also late. Who acts: travellers and corporate travel
  #" managers choosing an airport; small airports' air-service teams arguing their reliability to airlines. Caveat:
  #" small airports cancel more often and have fewer options when a flight is cancelled; the answer is "smaller
  #" airports are more often on time, hubs recover better", not one winner.
  view: small_airport_vs_hub is {
    group_by: size_band
    aggregate:
      airport_count is count(origin)
      flight_count, late_rate, cancel_rate, avg_arrival_delay, first_flight_share, chain_share
    order_by: size_band
  }

  #" PLAYBOOK "When should I fly out of <airport> to avoid delays?" Late rate and cancel rate by scheduled departure
  #" hour, with at least 50 flights per hour. Filter to one airport first, e.g.
  #" run: legs_summary -> best_departure_hour_from_airport + { where: origin = 'RDU' }. Who acts: business travellers
  #" and corporate travel managers (book the early hours, which leave before delays build up); airlines choosing
  #" where to add flights. For a specific route use route_hours_summary -> best_departure_window_by_route.
  view: best_departure_hour_from_airport is {
    group_by: dep_hour
    aggregate: flight_count, late_rate, cancel_rate, avg_arrival_delay
    having: flight_count >= 50
    order_by: dep_hour
  }
}

#" How delay carries through an aircraft's day (fast, 52,887 rows): flights by leg of the aircraft's day (leg 1 = first
#" flight, 7 = seventh or later) and, for later flights, whether the aircraft's first flight (day_start) and previous
#" flight (inbound) that day were late. Answers "how much more likely is a later flight to be late when the morning
#" flight was late?" Columns also include month_label, airline, origin, size_band.
source: legs_detail is motherduck.sql("""
  SELECT * FROM ltools.flights.flights_legs
""") extend {
  dimension:
    #" Leg of the aircraft's day: 1 = first flight, 7 = seventh or later.
    leg is chain_leg
    #" Later flights only: whether the aircraft's first flight of the day arrived 15+ minutes late.
    day_start is
      pick 'First flight of the day late' when chain_first_leg_late
      else 'First flight of the day on time'
    #" Later flights only: whether the aircraft's previous flight that day arrived 15+ minutes late.
    inbound is
      pick 'Inbound aircraft late' when chain_prev_leg_late
      else 'Inbound aircraft on time'
  measure:
    #" Flights that operated and have a tail number.
    legs is n_legs.sum()
    #" Of those, flights arriving 15+ minutes late.
    late_legs is n_late_legs.sum()
    #" Share arriving 15+ minutes late.
    # percent
    late_rate is late_legs / legs

  #" Later flights' late rate when the aircraft's first flight of the day was late vs on time.
  view: first_flight_effect is {
    where: leg > 1
    group_by: day_start
    aggregate: legs, late_rate
    order_by: day_start desc
  }

  #" Later flights' late rate when the aircraft's previous flight was late vs on time.
  view: inbound_effect is {
    where: leg > 1
    group_by: inbound
    aggregate: legs, late_rate
    order_by: inbound desc
  }

  #" Late rate by leg of the aircraft's day.
  view: late_by_leg is {
    group_by: leg
    aggregate: late_rate, legs
    order_by: leg
  }
}

#" START HERE for route questions (fast, 28,966 rows): flights per route ("RDU → ATL"; origin, dest), airline and
#" month. late_rate = share of operated flights arriving 15+ minutes late. For the best hour to fly a route, use
#" route_hours_summary.
source: routes_summary is motherduck.sql("""
  SELECT * FROM ltools.flights.flights_routes
""") extend {
  measure:
    #" Scheduled flights.
    flight_count is n_flights.sum()
    #" Share of operated flights arriving 15+ minutes late.
    # percent
    late_rate is n_late.sum() / n_operated.sum()
    #" Same, over flights with a tail number (matches legs_summary).
    # percent
    leg_late_rate is n_late_legs.sum() / n_legs.sum()
    #" Average arrival delay in minutes (early arrivals count as negative).
    avg_arrival_delay is arr_delay_min_sum.sum() / n_arr_delay.sum()

  #" Busy routes nationally (300+ flights) ranked by late rate. For one airport use least_reliable_routes instead
  #" (a refinement cannot lower this view's 300-flight threshold).
  view: worst_routes is {
    group_by: route
    aggregate: flight_count, late_rate, avg_arrival_delay
    having: flight_count >= 300
    order_by: late_rate desc
    limit: 15
  }

  #" PLAYBOOK "Which routes out of <airport> are least reliable?" Routes with 100+ flights in the selection, ranked
  #" by late rate. Filter to the airport: run: routes_summary -> least_reliable_routes + { where: origin = 'RDU' }.
  #" Who acts: travellers and travel managers (avoid, or allow a buffer); the airline and the two airports (fix the
  #" route). Then use route_hours_summary -> best_departure_window_by_route for the most reliable hour.
  view: least_reliable_routes is {
    group_by: route
    aggregate: flight_count, late_rate, avg_arrival_delay
    having: flight_count >= 100
    order_by: late_rate desc
    limit: 15
  }

  #" Same ranking with the tail-number late rate (top 10).
  view: worst_routes_legs is {
    group_by: route
    aggregate: flight_count, late_rate is leg_late_rate, avg_arrival_delay
    having: flight_count >= 300
    order_by: late_rate desc
    limit: 10
  }
}

#" Later flights of the day by the airport the aircraft flew in from (prev_origin), at each departure airport
#" (origin) (fast, 32,548 rows): how often the inbound aircraft was late (inbound_late_rate) and how often the flight
#" then left late. Answers "which inbound airports bring us late aircraft?"
source: inbound_summary is motherduck.sql("""
  SELECT * FROM ltools.flights.flights_inbound
""") extend {
  measure:
    #" Later flights (aircraft already flew today).
    legs is n_legs.sum()
    #" Share of those whose inbound flight (the aircraft's previous flight) arrived 15+ minutes late.
    # percent
    inbound_late_rate is n_inbound_late_legs.sum() / legs
    #" Share of those arriving 15+ minutes late themselves.
    # percent
    late_rate is n_late_legs.sum() / legs

  #" Inbound airports with 100+ later flights, by volume.
  view: inbound_from is {
    group_by: prev_origin
    aggregate: legs, inbound_late_rate, late_rate
    having: legs >= 100
    order_by: legs desc
    limit: 15
  }
}

#" Where delay chains that reach an airport START (fast, 39,986 rows). Each row counts late flights departing an
#" airport (origin = the airport AFFECTED) that were a later flight of an aircraft whose first flight of the day
#" left from ANOTHER airport (first_origin = where the chain STARTED) and arrived late. To answer "which airports
#" start the chains that reach RDU", filter origin = 'RDU' and rank first_origin (view
#" airports_whose_late_starts_reach_here). The airline column is the airline of the late flight at the affected
#" airport: add it to group_by to see whose network carries the chains in. Counts only; for dollar costs by starting
#" airport use chain_slots_summary.
source: chain_starts_summary is motherduck.sql("""
  SELECT * FROM ltools.flights.flights_chain_starts
""") extend {
  measure:
    #" Late later flights at the affected airport whose chain started elsewhere.
    late_legs is n_late_legs.sum()

  #" Top 10 starting airports (first_origin) by late flights they feed into the selection.
  view: chain_starts is {
    group_by: first_origin
    aggregate: late_legs
    order_by: late_legs desc
    limit: 10
  }

  #" PLAYBOOK "Which airports start the delay chains that reach <airport>?" Filter to the affected airport first:
  #" run: chain_starts_summary -> airports_whose_late_starts_reach_here + { where: origin = 'RDU' }. first_origin is
  #" where the aircraft's late morning flight started; share_of_chain_late_flights is its share of the affected
  #" airport's chain-late flights. Who acts: the affected airport's operations and air-service team (raise it with
  #" the airlines on those routes; ask for later first rotations or spare aircraft at the starting airport) and
  #" the airline (protect those first departures: chain_slots_summary -> departures_to_protect_first).
  view: airports_whose_late_starts_reach_here is {
    group_by: first_origin
    aggregate:
      late_legs
      # percent
      share_of_chain_late_flights is late_legs / all(late_legs)
    order_by: late_legs desc
    limit: 15
  }
}

#" START HERE for the cost of delay chains: which departures to protect, which airports and airlines they cost, and
#" what it is worth (fast, 4,497 rows; identical numbers to the slow `chain_slots` source).
#" A delay chain: an aircraft's first flight of the day arrives 15+ minutes late and its later flights that day log
#" "late aircraft" delay (BTS cause code). One row per SLOT = airline x first-flight departure airport (origin) x
#" scheduled departure hour (dep_hour, local) that started at least one chain, May-July 2026 (92 days).
#" COST IS A RANGE, ALWAYS QUOTE BOTH ENDS: conservative_cost_usd (only the NEXT flight's late-aircraft minutes, at
#" $69.07 a minute = A4A 2025 block-minute cost less fuel) to downstream_cost_usd (UPPER BOUND: every later flight's
#" minutes, at $100.76 a minute = A4A 2024 block-minute cost). Nationally $101.3M to $369.9M over the three months.
#" Caveats: attribution, not proof; storms delay first and later flights together, so not every minute was
#" avoidable. Airlines see their own propagation in more detail; this public view's value is that it covers every
#" airline at every airport, so airports, smaller carriers benchmarking peers, and corporate travel buyers can use it.
source: chain_slots_summary is motherduck.sql("""
  SELECT * FROM ltools.flights.chain_slots
""") extend {
  join_one: airport is airport_list on origin = airport.origin

  dimension:
    #" Departure time of the slot as a label, e.g. "7am".
    hour_label is
      pick '12am' when dep_hour = 0
      pick concat(dep_hour::"INTEGER"::string, 'am') when dep_hour < 12
      pick '12pm' when dep_hour = 12
      else concat((dep_hour - 12)::"INTEGER"::string, 'pm')
    #" The slot is among the 50 most costly nationally (upper-bound ranking).
    in_top_50 is slot_rank <= 50
    #" A morning first departure, scheduled 5:00-9:59am local (the first wave).
    is_morning is dep_hour >= 5 and dep_hour <= 9

  measure:
    #" Number of slots (airline x airport x hour) that started at least one late chain.
    slot_count is count()
    #" Aircraft-days whose first flight in this slot arrived 15+ minutes late and flew again that day.
    late_first_legs is slot_late_first_legs.sum()
    #" Later flights of those aircraft-days.
    downstream_legs is slot_downstream_legs.sum()
    #" UPPER-BOUND minutes: late-aircraft delay minutes on every later flight of those aircraft-days.
    downstream_late_aircraft_minutes is slot_minutes.sum()
    #" UPPER-BOUND cost in US dollars, May-July 2026: upper-bound minutes at $100.76 a minute (Airlines for America,
    #" 2024 average US passenger airline cost per block minute). Quote with conservative_cost_usd as a range.
    downstream_cost_usd is downstream_late_aircraft_minutes * 100.76
    #" CONSERVATIVE minutes (a): late-aircraft minutes on the next flight only.
    next_leg_late_aircraft_minutes is slot_next_leg_minutes.sum()
    #" CONSERVATIVE minutes (b): every later flight's minutes, capped per aircraft-day at the first flight's own
    #" arrival delay. A cross-check; usually between (a) and the upper bound.
    capped_late_aircraft_minutes is slot_capped_minutes.sum()
    #" CONSERVATIVE cost in US dollars, May-July 2026 (low end of the range): next-flight-only minutes at $69.07 a
    #" minute (A4A 2025 block-minute cost $98.41 less fuel $29.34; a plane held at the gate burns little fuel).
    conservative_cost_usd is next_leg_late_aircraft_minutes * 69.07
    #" Share of the (upper-bound) cost carried by the 50 most costly slots nationally.
    # percent
    top_50_share is downstream_late_aircraft_minutes { where: in_top_50 } / downstream_late_aircraft_minutes

  #" The 25 most costly slots nationally (upper-bound ranking), with both ends of the cost range.
  view: top_slots is {
    group_by: slot_rank, airline, origin, hour_label
    aggregate: late_first_legs, downstream_late_aircraft_minutes, conservative_cost_usd, downstream_cost_usd
    order_by: slot_rank
    limit: 25
  }

  #" Delay-chain cost by the airport where the chain started (upper bound, plus the conservative end).
  # bar_chart { x=origin y=downstream_cost_usd }
  view: cost_by_airport is {
    group_by: origin
    aggregate: downstream_cost_usd, conservative_cost_usd, late_first_legs
    order_by: downstream_cost_usd desc
    limit: 15
  }

  #" PLAYBOOK "Which departure slots cost the most in delay chains, and what should we do?" The 25 costliest
  #" airline x airport x hour first departures, nationally ranked (slot_rank upper bound; slot_rank_conservative under
  #" the next-flight-only method), with the cost range for May-July 2026. Filter to one airline or airport with
  #" + { where: airline = 'United' } or + { where: origin = 'CLT' } (ranks stay national). WHICH RANK TO ACT ON: start
  #" with slots high under BOTH ranks (e.g. American DFW 7am and 8am). A slot high only on slot_rank (e.g. Southwest
  #" BWI 6am, 7th vs 114th) has most of its cost on the third and later flights, so the fix is the whole day's rotation
  #" (turn buffers later in the day), not only the first departure. Who acts and what to do:
  #" the AIRLINE's operations control and network planning (hold a spare aircraft and crew at the top slots' airports,
  #" add turn buffer to those first rotations, give them priority at the gate and de-icing); the AIRPORT's operations
  #" team (gate, ground-handling and staffing priority for those departures; raise the list with the carrier). A
  #" smaller carrier can benchmark its slots against peers at the same airport and hour.
  view: departures_to_protect_first is {
    group_by: slot_rank, slot_rank_conservative, airline, origin, hour_label
    aggregate:
      late_first_legs
      conservative_cost_usd
      downstream_cost_usd
    order_by: slot_rank
    limit: 25
  }

  #" PLAYBOOK "Which morning departures should <airport> prioritise?" First-wave slots (scheduled 5-9am) at an
  #" airport, ranked by delay-chain cost. Filter to the airport first:
  #" run: chain_slots_summary -> morning_departures_to_prioritise + { where: origin = 'CLT' }. Who acts: the
  #" airport's operations centre (gate, tug, fuel, catering and security-lane priority for these departures) and the
  #" airline's station manager.
  view: morning_departures_to_prioritise is {
    where: is_morning
    group_by: slot_rank, airline, origin, hour_label
    aggregate: late_first_legs, conservative_cost_usd, downstream_cost_usd
    order_by: downstream_cost_usd desc
    limit: 15
  }

  #" PLAYBOOK "Which airports start the most costly delay chains?" Departure airports ranked by the delay-chain cost
  #" their late first flights of the day set off, with the cost range and the share of the total (national when
  #" unfiltered). Who acts:
  #" airport operations and air-service development (DFW, CLT, ORD and others at the top: first-wave reliability is
  #" what airlines weigh when adding service), and the airlines based there.
  view: airports_that_start_the_most_costly_chains is {
    group_by: origin, airport_city is airport.origin_city, size_band
    aggregate:
      late_first_legs
      conservative_cost_usd
      downstream_cost_usd
      #" Share of the upper-bound cost of everything in the query. Unfiltered, that is the national total; with a
      #" where: filter it is the share of the filtered total (so 100% for a single airline). Run unfiltered to compare.
      # percent
      share_of_total_cost is downstream_late_aircraft_minutes / all(downstream_late_aircraft_minutes)
    order_by: downstream_cost_usd desc
    limit: 20
  }

  #" PLAYBOOK "How much do delay chains cost <airline>?" Each airline's delay-chain cost range for May-July 2026, its
  #" share of the total (run unfiltered for national shares), and how concentrated it is (cost in its own 5 worst
  #" slots). Filter for one airline:
  #" + { where: airline = 'United' }. Regional carriers (SkyWest, Envoy, Republic, Endeavor, PSA) are listed under
  #" their own names, not the mainline brand they fly for. Who acts: the airline's operations and network planning;
  #" for a buyer comparing carriers, the share_of_total_cost column (unfiltered).
  view: delay_chain_cost_by_airline is {
    group_by: airline
    aggregate:
      slot_count
      late_first_legs
      conservative_cost_usd
      downstream_cost_usd
      #" Share of the upper-bound cost of everything in the query. Unfiltered, that is the national total; with a
      #" where: filter it is the share of the filtered total (so 100% for a single airline). Run unfiltered to compare.
      # percent
      share_of_total_cost is downstream_late_aircraft_minutes / all(downstream_late_aircraft_minutes)
    nest: worst_5_slots is {
      group_by: slot_rank, origin, hour_label
      aggregate: conservative_cost_usd, downstream_cost_usd
      order_by: slot_rank
      limit: 5
    }
    order_by: downstream_cost_usd desc
  }

  #" PLAYBOOK "What is the total cost of delay chains, and how sure are we?" The national (or filtered) total as a
  #" range, with all three minute counts so the method can be checked: upper bound (every later flight, $100.76/min),
  #" capped at the first flight's own delay, and next flight only ($69.07/min = the conservative cost).
  view: delay_chain_cost_range is {
    aggregate:
      slot_count, late_first_legs
      next_leg_late_aircraft_minutes, capped_late_aircraft_minutes, downstream_late_aircraft_minutes
      conservative_cost_usd, downstream_cost_usd
  }

  #" Delay-chain cost by the scheduled hour of the late first flight (the early bank sets up the day).
  view: chain_cost_by_departure_hour is {
    group_by: dep_hour, hour_label
    aggregate: late_first_legs, conservative_cost_usd, downstream_cost_usd
    order_by: dep_hour
  }
}

#" START HERE for "what time should I fly route X?" (fast, ~115k rows): flights per route, airline, month and
#" scheduled departure hour (dep_hour, local). Columns: month_label, carrier, airline, origin, dest, route
#" ("RDU → ATL"), size_band, dep_hour. late_rate = share of operated flights arriving 15+ minutes late.
source: route_hours_summary is motherduck.sql("""
  SELECT * FROM ltools.flights.flights_route_hours
""") extend {
  measure:
    #" Scheduled flights.
    flight_count is n_flights.sum()
    #" Share of scheduled flights cancelled.
    # percent
    cancel_rate is n_cancelled.sum() / n_flights.sum()
    #" Share of operated flights arriving 15+ minutes late.
    # percent
    late_rate is n_late.sum() / n_operated.sum()
    #" Average arrival delay in minutes (early arrivals count as negative).
    avg_arrival_delay is arr_delay_min_sum.sum() / n_arr_delay.sum()

  #" PLAYBOOK "What is the best time of day to fly this route?" For each busy route (90+ flights), its overall late
  #" rate and its three most reliable departure hours (20+ flights each). Filter first, e.g.
  #" run: route_hours_summary -> best_departure_window_by_route + { where: origin = 'RDU' } (or route = 'RDU → ATL').
  #" The three hours are the best AVAILABLE on that route, not necessarily good: compare each with the route's own
  #" late_rate, and treat hours with 20-30 flights as noisy. Lists the 50 busiest routes in the selection; for a
  #" smaller route filter by route. Who acts: business travellers and corporate travel managers (book those hours;
  #" add buffer otherwise); airlines planning which hours to add capacity.
  view: best_departure_window_by_route is {
    group_by: route
    aggregate: flight_count, late_rate
    having: flight_count >= 90
    nest: most_reliable_hours is {
      group_by: dep_hour
      aggregate: flight_count, late_rate
      having: flight_count >= 20
      order_by: late_rate
      limit: 3
    }
    order_by: flight_count desc
    limit: 50
  }
}
